      "Title": "Role of Autophagy in Peripheral Nerve Development and Repair",
      "Title2": null,
      "Abstract": "Peripheral nerve myelination and injury-induced demyelination involve dynamic changes in Schwann cell morphology and membrane structure. Recent studies have demonstrated that autophagy regulates organelle biogenesis and plasma membrane dynamics. In the present study, I was investigated the role of autophagy in the development and differentiation of myelinating Schwann cells during postnatal development. In addition, the function of Schwann cell autophagy in demyelination during Wallerian degeneration was examined. Electron microscopy and biochemical assays have shown that Schwann cells activate macroautophagy to remove excess cytoplasmic organelles during myelination. Inhibition of autophagy via Schwann cell-specific removal of atg7 (autophagy-related gene 7) gene, an essential gene for macroautophagy, using a conditional knockout strategy, resulted in abnormally enlarged abaxonal cytoplasm of Schwann cells. Rapamycin-induced suppression of mTOR activity during the early postnatal period enhanced not only autophagy but also developmental reduction of cytoplasm of myelinating Schwann cells in vivo. The activation of autophagolysosomes was induced in demyelinating Schwann cells after injury, and the inhibition of autophagy in Schwann cell-specific manner and pharmacological intervention of lysosomal function caused a significant but a minor delay in demyelination. Schwann cell dedifferentiation, as demonstrated by ERK activation and c-jun induction, and redifferentiation were not significantly affected by Schwann cell autophagy, and thus the entire repair program progressed normally in Schwann cell specific-atg7 knockout mice. In contrast, macrophage appeared to determine most myelin lipid and protein clearance during Wallerian degeneration. Furthermore, autophagy-defective macrophages showed significant loss of ability to regulate lipid salvage during nerve repair, and thus macrophage autophagy-defective mice revealed a significantly delayed Schwann cell redifferentiation and nerve repair. Taken together, this study suggests that autophagy is developmental and injury responses of Schwann cells to modulate their membrane structures and that macrophage autophagy is more critical to myelin digestion and nerve repair than Schwann cell autophagy after injury.",
      "Abstract2": null,
      "Keyword": [
