    {
      "CN": "DIKO0013724303",
      "DBCode": "DIKO",
      "Title": "Enhanced neuronal differentiation and neurite outgrowth by intracellular electrical stimulation through vertical nanowire electrode array",
      "Title2": null,
      "Abstract": "In this thesis, it is demonstrated that intracellular electrical stimulation of Nerve Growth Factor (NGF) treated PC-12 cells, which are representative model of neuronal differentiation, through vertical SiNW electrodes. Vertical SiNW electrodes were prepared by firstly growth of SiNWs on Si wafers by vapor-liquid-solid (VLS) mechanism and then fabricated as electrodes by using semiconductor fine processing. I then cultured PC-12 cells on Poly-L-Lysin (PLL) coated SiNW electrodes with 105/cm2 density for 4 days with intracellular electrical stimulation (constant 50mV) and 2 days of stabilizing period. The morphological differentiation of PC-12 cells were observed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). It was found that more PC-12 cells were cultured on NWs (about 4 times more dense) than the flat-films. It was also found that intracellular electrical stimulation with SiNW electrodes induce neural outgrowth and are effective in differentiation (40 um longer in average neurite length) compare to extracellular stimulation with Au films. PC-12 cells on SiNW electrodes with intracellular electrical stimulation were about 52% more grown in neural outgrowth than cells on the same device without electrical stimulation, and about 75% more grown in neural outgrowth than cells on Au thin film device with extracellular electrical stimulation. Mechanisms underlying the intracellular response to electrical stimulation is yet unclear, but the resting potential of neurons is ？60 to -70 mV, and intracellular electrical stimulation through SiNW electrodes makes increase in resting potential state up to threshold potential constantly Then, β-actin, NF-66, and Tuj-1 were quantified using western blot analysis. Increase in protein expression of NF-66 during NGF-induced differentiation of PC-12 cells could support the above idea that intracellular electrical stimulation corroborate with NGF enhances genetic cellular differentiation of PC-12 cells. The PC-12 cells cultured on SiNW electrodes were further characterized by z-step fluorescent scanning in two-photon microscopy. It showed that the cells were pierced by NWs that clearly demonstrate the intracellular mode electrical stimulations of the cells, i.e., SiNWs penetrate a cell’s membrane, and subsequently release electrical stimulation directly into the cell’s cytosol, and allows highly efficient stimulation. Our results indicate that SiNW electrodes could be a new way of stimulation of neuron cell efficiently under living states for a long time.",
      "Abstract2": null,
