    {
      "CN": "DIKO0017169745",
      "DBCode": "DIKO",
      "Title": "폐식용유 바이오연료를 활용한 연소 및 배출가스 최적화에 관한 실험적 연구",
      "Title2": null,
      "Abstract": "The growing concerns about environmental pollution and the urgent need for sustainable energy solutions have accelerated the adoption of biofuel as an alternative to fossil fuels. Biofuel produced from waste cooking oil (WCO) offers significant environmental benefits, including reduced greenhouse gas emissions and effective repurposing of waste materials. These advantages contribute to addressing global energy challenges and position biofuel as a practical solution for mitigating the environmental impacts of traditional pure diesel use.&#xD; This study evaluates the performance and emission characteristics of WCO biofuel compared to pure diesel (D100) and other biofuels under various operating conditions. In the first phase, WCO B20 demonstrated reductions in CO, HC, and PM emissions by approximately 40%, 20%, and 25%, respectively, compared to D100, while NOx emissions increased by 20%. In the second phase, under 0%, 5%, and 10% load conditions, biofuel blends exhibited significant emission reductions, with SOB biofuel showing more balanced performance than WCO. The third phase introduced Al₂O₃ nanoparticles(50ppm and 100ppm), which further enhanced emission reductions and improved combustion performance, reducing CO and HC emissions by up to 60% and 52%, respectively, and mitigating NOx by 55% at higher nanoparticle concentrations.&#xD; The findings confirm the environmental advantages of WCO biofuel, particularly when combined with Al₂O₃ nanoadditives, and highlight its potential as a viable alternative to fossil fuels. These results provide a foundation for optimizing biofuel blends and nanoparticle additives for enhanced sustainability and performance in diesel engines.",
