    {
      "CN": "DIKO0017285623",
      "DBCode": "DIKO",
      "Title": "구기자나무(Lycium chinense Mill.) 뿌리껍질 추출물 및 비타민 D가 키 성장 관련 인자에 미치는 효과 연구",
      "Title2": "Effects of Lycium chinense Mill. Root Bark Extract and Vitamin D on Height Growth-Related Factor",
      "Abstract": "Growth is an indicator of a children’s overall health, and short stature can negatively impact not only physical development but also quality of life and mental well-being. As a result, there is a growing interest in improving children's growth disorders worldwide. The GH-IGF-1 axis is a key endocrine pathway in growth regulation and is precisely regulated by growth hormone (GH), insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1), and insulin-like growth factor-binding protein-3 (IGFBP-3). Currently, GH injection is used as a representative treatment for growth disorders, but there are limitations such as side effects, high cost, and psychological burden. Therefore, it is necessary to develop a safer and more economical new treatment.&#xD; Vitamin D is well known as a key nutrient for calcium homestasis and bone metabolism, and Lycii radicis cortex (LRC) has been reported to have antioxidant, anti-diabetic, anti-inflammatory, and anti-osteoporosis effects. However, little has been reported about the impact on height growth. Therefore, in this study we inverstigated the effects of LRC extract and vitamin D, either alone or in combination, on the expression of growth-related factors in HepG2 cells and 8-week-old C57BL/6J mice.&#xD; Based on the results of the MTT assay, HepG2 cells were treated with vitamin D(1,25(OH)2D3) at a concentration of 10 nM and LRC extract at concentrations of 10 and 50 μg/mL. Each concentration of LRC extract was also co-treated with vitamin D. The animal experiment was designed based on previous studieis, using LRC extract at 300 mg/kg, a combination of LRC 150 mg/kg and vitamin D(cholecalciferol) 500 IU, and a combination of LRC 300 mg/kg and vitamin D 500 IU. Recombinant human growth hormone (rhGH) and Kiker Gold were used as positive controls for comparison.&#xD; In HepG2 cells, RT-qPCR and Western blot analyses showed that IGF-1 mRNA expression did not significantly increase, although a tendency to increase was observed in the vitamin D alone group. In contrast, IGF-1 protein levels were significantly elevated in the LRC 50 μg/mL group, the VD + LRC 10 μg/mL group, and the VD + LRC 50 μg/mL group. IGFBP-1 mRNA expression was significantly increased in the VD + LRC 50 μg/mL group, while both mRNA and protein levels of IGFBP-3 were significantly increased in the combination treatment groups. GHR mRNA expression showed no significant change in any of the treatment groups.&#xD; In mouse liver tissue, there were no significant changes in IGF-1 and IGFBP-1 mRNA expression in most groups; however, IGF-1 mRNA expression was significantly increased in the LRC and vitamin D combination groups compared to the Kiker Gold group. IGFBP-3 mRNA expression was significantly elevated in the LRC 300 mg/kg alone group and the LRC 150 mg/kg + vitamin D group. Protein analysis showed consistent results: IGF-1 protein levels were significantly increased in both co-administered groups, and IGFBP-3 protein levels were significantly elevated in the LRC 150 mg/kg + vitamin D group. GHR mRNA expression showed no significant changes in any of the administration groups.&#xD; Overall, LRC extract alone enhanced the expression of certain growth-related factors at both the mRNA and protein levels, and this effect was further augmented by co-administration with vitamin D.&#xD; As a result of the evaluation of growth indicators, the body size and weight of mice gradually increased significantly during the experimental period, and the largest increase was observed at 3 week of the experiment, especially in the LRC 300 mg/kg and vitamin D co-administered groups. However, in the thickness of the tibia growth plate, there was no significant structural change in all groups. Calcium and phosphorus concentrations in mouse serum remained stable without significant changes in all administration groups, suggesting that LRC extract and vitamin D could induce the expression of growth-related factors without interfering with mineral homeostasis in the body.&#xD; In conclusion this study shows that LRC extract and vitamin D can have a positive effect on growth promotion through regulation of gene and protein expression related to the IGF-1 axis. In particular, when the LRC extract and vitamin D are combined, the effect is more pronounced, suggesting the possibility of a synergy effect through the interaction of the two substances. These results provide basic data that co-treatment of LRC extract and vitamin D can be used as functional materials for promoting growth.",
