    {
      "CN": "DIKO0017284896",
      "DBCode": "DIKO",
      "Title": "유전자편집 기술을 이용한 콩 등숙 유전자 E1 돌연변이 계통 개발 및 농업적 특성 구명",
      "Title2": "Development of soybean maturity gene E1 mutant lines using genome editing technology and characterization of their agricultural traits",
      "Abstract": "Soybean (Glycine max L.), a major global source of plant-derived protein and oil, exhibits high sensitivity to photoperiod, which regulates its flowering time, maturation, and environmental adaptation. The limited photoperiodic responsiveness of current soybean cultivars restricts their regional cultivation range and yield potential. To enhance latitude adaptability and maximize productivity, modulation of flowering and maturation timing is essential. Among the 11 major genes (E1 through E10 and J) known to regulate flowering time and maturity in soybean, The E1 gene was targeted using a CRISPR/Cas9-mediated genome editing (GE) approach. From 31 T₀ transgenic lines, two lines (#4 and #6) showing strong editing signals in InDel PCR were selected. Targeted deep sequencing confirmed that both lines carried premature stop codons as predicted by the gRNA design. In the T₁ generation, two lines (#4-2 and #6-15) with high editing efficiency were further selected. Bar PCR and TaqMan PCR analyses indicated the presence of a single T-DNA insertion in these lines. In the T₂ generation, GmE1 GE plants exhibited earlier flowering and accelerated maturation compared to the wild-type control (Williams 82). Notably, in the T₃ generation, we identified a line (#6-15-3-6) that flowered 10 days earlier under long-day (16h light/8h dark) chamber conditions, which typically delay flowering in soybean. Targeted deep sequencing revealed that this line harbors a single nucleotide deletion that results in a premature stop codon in the E1 gene, with high editing efficiency. In the T₄ generation, we obtained a GmE1 GE line with 100% editing. When cultivated under both long-day and short-day (12h/12h light/dark) conditions, this line consistently exhibited a shortened flowering period compared to Williams 82. Furthermore, we conducted a comparative analysis under identical growth conditions among GmE1 GE, GmE2 ST GE, and GmE2/E2 h1 CT GE lines to evaluate differences in flowering and maturation phenotypes. This study demonstrates the effectiveness of genome editing at the E loci, particularly E1, in generating soybean lines with desirable flowering and maturation traits tailored to diverse environmental conditions.",
