    {
      "CN": "DIKO0016929360",
      "DBCode": "DIKO",
      "Title": "Structural and biochemical studies of the bovine mitochondrial respirasome and its ubiquinone channeling mechanism",
      "Title2": null,
      "Abstract": "When macromolecules are catabolized for energy in the Krebs cycle, the end products converge into the mitochondrial electron transport chain, the final stage of ATP production. Electrons passed on from molecules such as NADH or succinate pass through an array of complex protein machineries which use the energy from these electrons to pump hydrogen ions from the mitochondrial matrix to the mitochondrial intermembrane space. The consequential hydrogen ion concentration disparity between the two sides of the mitochondrial inner membrane is the energy used to phosphorylate ADP into ATP. The machineries involved in this electron transport are complex I, complex II, complex III and complex IV. Though their structure has been revealed to atomic resolution levels, the exact mechanisms in detail still remain an enigma. The most controversial part of this enigma is the reason for the existence of the supercomplex. Complex I, III and IV are known to form a supercomplex structure known as respirasome. however, the advantage of assembling into this superstructure is heavily argued. One side argues that the respirasome provides not only structural stability and higher activity through diffusion distance reduction, but also substrate channeling. The other side disagrees with the presence of substrate channeling. Supercomplex formation is hindered in many diseases, highlighting the importance of supercomplex formation. Unlike previous respirasome structures, I have purified a respirasome structure in amphipol instead of digitonin. When its structure was observed through cryo-EM, I found several notable differences compared to the previous structures purified in digitonin. The first difference was that the respirasome had different states where the position of complex III2 and complex IV shifted between different states. I hypothesized that by replacing digitonin with amphipol after solubilization, we have purified a more native respirasome with more lipids embedded in the intercomplex region, which is why the respirasome showed different states. In this present study, we show that this respirasome not only showed more lipids, but also a higher overall activity as well. Another difference we noticed was that that the respirasome shows a cavity branching from its previously known cavity into the space between complex I and complex III. This novel cavity may be the tunnel used to channel ubiquinone between complex I and complex III .In order to confirm whether this channel is actually traveled by the ubiquinone, I put the respirasome structure in a turnover state actively engaging in electron transport and viewed its structure through cryo- EM microscopy. This structure highlighted not only the sites and mechanisms of the electron transport within the respirasome, but also a density of an ubiquinone in the novel cavity of respirasome.",
      "Abstract2": null,
      "Keyword": [
        "생명공학"
